Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.589 align:start position:0%
 
over the weekend afghan government  troops in the north of the country  abandoned their posts  and fled to neighboring tajikistan the  reason  a taliban advanced they felt they were  ill-equipped to oppose  it's a pattern playing out across the  country with the taliban now controlling  some one-third of the country's 421  districts  these gains come as international troops  continue to pull out from the country  a scenario that's left many afghans  worried about their future  these afghan people are determined to  leave the country  that's why thousands of them have  gathered at this passport office in  capital kabul  trying to find a way out  their future has become too uncertain as  the effect of the us troops withdrawal  starts to unfold  most of the districts have fallen  there's not a single district without  fighting  the taliban have even reached the chief  of police's office and provincial  capitals  i've come here to get my passport and  get out of afghanistan  it's not safe here many  embassies have registered a dramatic  increase in the number of visa and  passport applications  as the taliban advances people fear the  hard-won  progress over the past two decades will  start to roll back  women and girls for example went from  near total exclusion from public life  to being able to work and study in mixed  schools today  now these gains are hanging in the  balance  very recently we have reports that as  districts that have been taken over by  the taliban  they have already imposed restrictions  on women on media for instance women  are in in tahar reportedly not allowed  to leave the house without a male  chaperone clinics have been closed  there's also  reports of media blackout this is  happening  despite the taliban's promises that the  public has  nothing to fear  and the situation could intensify  the group has already made significant  territorial gains  in the past months  afghan forces are struggling to hold  them off  now our people thinking if govern our  government cannot to control  this situation maybe the civil war will  be stored so that's the main problem why  people  trying to go abroad  those who couldn't afford a visa are  seeking to leave illegally  many of them are young people  they're about to cross the border to  iran before embarking on further  journeys  hoping to secure a better future  one section of afghan citizens that's  particularly concerned about its future  are its women  and for more on that i'm joined by aisha  khoram a law student at kabul university  and a former afghan youth representative  to the u.n aisha nearly 50 of  afghanistan's population is  women how worried are they about life  under possible taliban influence  well if we look at the rapid  and unconditional withdrawal of the used  troops and  since the announcement of united states  and nato  troops which are all from afghanistan we  can see that  the slightest hope for peace also  vanished  and right now there is a windless  mentality on the ground and taliban are  getting more territory  and it's leaving a lot of people  uh not only women but all afghanistan is  worried about what is to come  um and now the future of the peace  process the future of afghanistan and  the younger generation is  it's all in in ambiguity it's in  question  now taliban as you correctly point out  have rapidly advanced across the country  from what you  are hearing can you describe for us what  the situation is particularly for women  in the areas that they control  well women right now women living in  some of the districts uh  their situation was relatively uh  better since 2001 however there is a  huge gap of opportunities and facilities  when it comes to urban and rural areas  of afghanistan  but the relative success and the  relative  security the relative progress that  women had even in the forest  district of afghanistan it was something  to be preserved  and protected and right now um as  taliban are gaining more territory as um  the government is staying silent about  it and as the united states is  withdrawing irresponsibly it all makes  um women more frightened  of what is to come and right now we are  left with no guarantee  of any future that we could look forward  to now we are also getting reports that  the  taliban are being welcomed in some of  the areas  that they are capturing what explains  this support  for the taliban  well in some of the districts  that are um right now and the taliban's  control  over the years uh when there were  airstrikes and where they were  some of the war crimes committed by  revolving government and the  international community  it really created this um this uh  sense of uh peop among people that they  could  uh rely on on a community  uh on on the people who are from those  communities who  are rising against the government but  does not particularly mean they are  supporting talwan's ideology  you see in the past 20 years a  generation was raised and born in this  conflict  now they even forgot what this war even  started at the beginning  at the first place and right now they  are fighting  even not knowing for what reason so  some of the people are even joining one  or another side  just for the sake of survival talk to me  a bit about  the conservative nature of afghan  society i mean how much of a priority  is girls education or women's  participation in politics for example  among the general population  well in 20 years ago when united states  and nato  started engagement military engagement  in afghanistan i was one year old  born as a refugee in peshawar and i  returned to afghanistan after 2001 with  my family i  was a child i grew up here i went to  school i went  to university i'm still at university  and you see that back then when  everything started  from from the very beginning of we were  building from the scratch we were  building from the ashes there was a lot  of hope a lot of optimism  and women were accomplished in the  society but today  everything is unquestioned including the  future of women's education and the most  concerning of all is  the increased level of violence but  besides that even during the peace  process  the language about the human rights and  the rights of women in the post peace  afghanistan was ambiguous and that was  a very concerning point for a lot of  women  we leave it there for the time being but  thank you so much for joining us today  and afghanistan's neighbor pakistan has  long been seen as having influence over  the taliban  for that reason pakistan's involvement  in the peace process  is seen as crucial to its outcome  relations between pakistan and the  taliban go  way back the group itself was formed in  the early 90s  primarily by pashtun students in  pakistani madrasas  or islamic religious schools pakistan  was one of only  three countries that recognized the  taliban when it ruled afghanistan  between 1996 and 2001 and in the last  few years  pakistan has played a key role in  bringing the taliban  to the talks table for more let's head  to correspondent  jalani in karachi in pakistan she said  aside from pakistan's influence over the  taliban  what is driving pakistani engagement  with the afghan peace process  viresh the conventional wisdom around  the world is that  because of pakistan's deep involvement  in afghanistan over the last  four decade decades and its closeness to  the taliban  taliban are often seen as pakistan's  proxies  but what we are hearing now from  pakistani army leadership  is that they are losing influence with  taliban  last week the pakistani army chief and  the head of its  isi the intelligence agency was briefing  a panel of parliamentarians and they  were told  that they don't have as much influence  with taliban  as people seem to think and there's  growing anxiety within pakistan  that if things go haywire next door  pakistan will face  a really difficult time so we've also  heard from other think tanks like the  